+### Create-or-update form controllers
+
+`PetController` and `VisitController` each serve one form from two routes: a create route, and an edit route naming the entity to change. A single `@ModelAttribute` method supplies the bound entity for both, taking the identifier as an optional path variable. It returns a new instance when the identifier is absent, and otherwise navigates from the owner to the existing one. A new visit is attached to its pet; a resolved one is returned as it stands. A correction adds no second visit to the pet. Both routes render the same create-or-update template, which carries no action attribute and posts back to the URL that served it.
+
+Nothing in the owner–pet–visit graph is reached by its own identifier alone. A nested entity is resolved by navigating from the owner, matching the absent `PetRepository` and `VisitRepository`. The bound entity is re-resolved on every request rather than carried across one. Identifier binding is disallowed on the data binder; an identifier reaches a handler only from the path.
+
 ### Persistence
 
 The relational schema is not generated from the JPA mapping. `spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto` is set to `none`, and the schema is created by the per-vendor `schema.sql` scripts under `src/main/resources/db/`, with seed rows from the matching `data.sql`. The domain classes carry the JPA mapping annotations directly — there is no separate persistence model and no mapper between them. Under `architecture-principles.md` this is the sanctioned direct-mapping case: the project owns both the model and the schema, and the stored shape tracks the model closely.
